<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="UDP-α-D-glucuronate biosynthesis (from myo-inositol)" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-4841" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Accept-If-Predicted Pathways (AIPP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are present widely throughout the plant kingdom. The AIPP list contains pathways that are automatically accepted if they are predicted for any plant species database. However, AIPP pathways are not automatically imported into databases if they are not predicted by the Pathologic program. &lt;A href=&quot;https://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; In plants, a large amount of the cell-wall biomass is derived from the precursor UDP-glucuronate (UDP-GlcA) through the complex interconversion of nucleotide sugars (for review see |CITS:[15134748]|). UDP-GlcA is converted to a number of other sugars including UDP-galacturonate, UDP-xylose and UDP-arabinose. UDP-GlcA is synthesized in plants via two independent routes. The more predominant route involves the direct oxidation of UDP-glucose by UDP-glucose dehydrogenase (see |FRAME:EC-1.1.1.22|). A second pathway (this pathway) however has been described in plants and relies on the oxygenation of &lt;i&gt;myo&lt;/i&gt;-inositol. Free &lt;i&gt;myo&lt;/i&gt;-inositol is synthesized from glucose-6-phosphate, phytate or acquired from exogenous sources. &lt;b&gt;Key Notes on the Enzymes:&lt;/b&gt; Within the inositol oxygenase pathway, &lt;i&gt;myo&lt;/i&gt;-inositol is converted into free GlcA by &lt;i&gt;myo&lt;/i&gt;-inositol oxygenase (MIOX, EC 1.13.99.1). This enzyme is a key committing step in the pathway as it catalyzes the thermodynamically irreversible oxygenative cleavage of &lt;i&gt;myo&lt;/i&gt;-inositol to GlcA. The second step of the pathway involves the conversion of GlcA into GlcA 1-phosphate. This reaction is catalyzed by a glucuronokinase in the presence of ATP and Mg&lt;sup&gt;2+&lt;/sup&gt;. This catalytic activity has been reported in several plants (for example, |FRAME:TAX-4577|, |FRAME:TAX-4097|, and |FRAME:TAX-3847| |CITS:[DICKINSON82]|). It was first characterized in more detail only in |FRAME:TAX-3916| and &lt;i&gt;Lilium longifolium&lt;/i&gt; |CITS:[192151],[13661996]|, but work on the latter enzyme eventually allowed for the identification of a full-length functional |FRAME: AT3G01640-MONOMER| in |FRAME:ORG-5993| |CITS: [19951951]|. The last step of the pathway is catalyzed by enzymes with UDP-glucuronate pyrophosphate activity. One enzyme partially purified from barley seedlings was shown to catalyze the conversion of GlcA 1-phosphate into UDP-GlcA |CITS:[5570433]|. The study of this enzyme suggested that the production of UDP-GlcA from &lt;i&gt;myo&lt;/i&gt;-inositol might be the predominant route in young barley seedlings. A second enzyme isolated from pea sprouts was also shown to catalyze this reaction. Interestingly, this enzyme was able to convert a wide range of monosaccharides 1-phosphate to their activated UDP-sugar form. This enzyme was postulated to be involved in the formation of UDP-sugars at the end of salvage pathways in higher plants |CITS:[15326166]|.</Description>
